      <!-- Closing -->
      <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The <strong class="text-white">silane versus siloxane</strong> question is really a question about your substrate. Silane's smaller molecule penetrates dense, low-porosity concrete; siloxane suits porous block, brick, and older concrete; and the silane-siloxane blends that dominate the market exist precisely because they cover both.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          For most homeowners a quality blend is the right default. Confirm the surface actually absorbs water before you buy, apply until it stops absorbing rather than to a coverage figure, and remember what this whole family does not do — it will not add gloss, and it will not stop oil staining.
        </p>
